##			- Added commands: scommit and scompul.
##			- Changed all 'git pull' to 'git pull --ff-only'.
##		- 20220502 JC: Updated.
##		- 20260722 JC:
##			- Rewrote on the current template generation: strict mode, arg parser, trap suite, minified header trio.
##			- Every command now verifies repo state first and is idempotent: stash only if dirty (and pop only what was pushed), pull only with an upstream, push only if ahead, commit only if there are changes.
##			- Detect main vs master from origin's HEAD instead of a hardcoded name.
##			- Run git via argument arrays instead of eval; dropped the curly-quote message mangling (no longer needed).
##			- mtm merges --no-ff and deletes the merged branch local + remote; guards against running from the default branch.
##			- mkbranch/chbranch validate branch existence up front and park current work (commit+pull+push) before switching.
##			- Added -q/--quiet and -m/--message/--msg; message can still be given positionally.
##			- Removed the unused template library (~1400 lines).
##		- 20260722 JC:
##			- Renamed commands: scompul->saveup, spush->sync, scommit->commit, spull->pull, mkbranch->newbr, chbranch->gobr, list->listbr, mtm->land. Old names still work (hidden aliases).
##			- newbr/gobr/land now target dev when the repo has one, else main/master.
##			- Added pr (list / review / accept, via gh) and release (merge dev into main, tag, push; bumps patch when no version given).
##		- 20260723 JC:
##			- Pre-flight display before the confirm prompt: resolved SSH identity (alias -> real host, user, key), commit author, ahead/behind, and the files an incoming pull would change.
##			- Changed files now list one per line, truncated to the terminal and capped, instead of git status's long form.
##		- 20260723 JC:
##			- Renamed saveup->update (saveup stays as a hidden alias).
##			- release now fast-forwards dev to main afterward so dev includes the release merge and tag; skipped (with a warning) if dev moved mid-release.
##			- Output starts with a blank line for breathing room after the shell prompt.
##		- 20260724 JC:
##			- pull now uses --autostash, so a failed pull leaves the working tree intact instead of leaving work in the stash.
##			- land publishes an upstream-less merge target before deleting the remote work branch.
##			- newbr carries dirty work from main/dev to the new branch instead of committing it there; gobr refuses to auto-commit on those branches.
##		- 20260724 JC:
##			- -h/-v only recognized in the command slot, so a message like "add -v flag" no longer short-circuits the run.
##			- Non-tty mutating runs fail closed without -q; a stray extra word after a message is rejected instead of dropped.
##			- git failures report as a plain one-liner (the trap dump stays for real script errors); printf instead of echo -e so escape bytes in filenames/messages stay inert.
##			- Fetch now prunes, heals origin/HEAD, and gets an ssh connect timeout; --no-fetch skips it entirely.
##			- Displayed remote URLs mask embedded credentials; ssh -G gets --; command -v instead of which; release tolerates short tags like v1.2.
##			- land's remote-branch delete is non-fatal if the branch is already gone; default branch and merge target resolve once per run.
##		- 20260724 JC:
##			- newbr/gobr branch arguments validate before the preview, so a bad name can't show a nonsense plan.
##			- Bare 'help'/'version' words work; -y/--yes aliases for -q; usage errors drop the call-stack line.
##			- fIsAhead stops at the first commit; tag lookup via mapfile instead of a head pipeline.
##		- 20260724 JC:
##			- Added clone (get an existing repo; checks out dev if it has one; re-run is a no-op) and connect (publish local work: init if needed, commit, then push to an empty remote - or create the GitHub repo via gh for owner/name targets; --public/--private pick visibility).
##			- connect refuses remotes that already have history and won't change an existing origin; both new commands preview and confirm like everything else.
##		- 20260725 JC:
##			- release publishes an upstream-less default branch instead of pushing only the tag.
##			- The duplicate-tag and dirty-protected-branch refusals moved up front, so neither can fire after the plan was confirmed (and, for release, after it had already committed and pushed).
##			- newbr's plan no longer promises a commit when run from main/dev, where the tree is carried over instead.
##			- pr ok lands on the merge target when gh's --delete-branch removed the branch we were standing on, instead of pulling a branch the remote no longer has.
##			- A bare release after a candidate proposes that candidate's own version (v2.0.0-rc1 -> v2.0.0) rather than bumping past it.
##		- 20260726 JC:
##			- Added 'pr new [title]': parks the work, then opens a PR against the merge target. No title given, the last commit subject is used; an already-open PR for the branch reports its number instead of erroring.
##			- pr ok refuses a dirty tree or unpushed commits. gh's --delete-branch drops the branch local and remote, so anything not on origin was outside both the PR and the merge.
##		- 20260726 JC:
##			- Regrouped the infrequent commands under nouns: newbr/gobr/listbr/land -> br create/switch/list/land, clone/connect -> repo clone/connect, pr new -> pr create. 'repository'/'branch' spell out; 'new'/'go' stay as hidden subcommand aliases.
##			- Split the old connect in two: 'repo create <owner/name>' is the only thing that will bring a remote into existence, and 'repo connect' now refuses a target that doesn't exist yet.
##			- Dropped every pre-v2 command alias (scommit, spull, scompul, saveup, spush, mkbranch, chbranch, mtm, list). v2 is a clean break, under a new name.
##		- 20260726 JC:
##			- Dropped the bare 'commit' and 'pull' commands; update is the one command that does both. Each was a way around the workflow the tool enforces.
##			- update/sync now pull BEFORE committing. Committing first minted a local commit, so any remote that had moved ahead was diverged and the ff-only pull refused - the everyday case, and broken until now. 'pull' had been the accidental workaround.
##			- An unreachable remote warns and skips the pull instead of failing (update is the only way to commit now); --no-fetch means offline and skips the pull too. A reachable remote that can't fast-forward still fails hard.
##		- 20260726 JC:
##			- Every command's pull honors offline now, not just update/sync's - br create/switch/land, pr ok and release called git pull directly and ignored --no-fetch.
##			- The dirty-protected-branch refusal names 'update'; it still named the dropped 'commit'. Help text back in step with the command set.
##		- 20260726 JC:
##			- Added 'br prune': deletes every local branch already merged into the merge target, plus its remote copy. Nothing else cleaned up after a PR merged elsewhere, or after an abandoned branch.
##			- Unmerged branches are kept and listed, never deleted, and there is no --force.
##			- Deletes with -D behind gitsby's own containment check: 'git branch -d' asks about the upstream, or HEAD when there is none, so it warned needlessly on one branch shape and silently refused another that was genuinely merged.
##			- A branch kept by the delete-time re-check keeps its remote copy too, and a merged current branch is reported as kept rather than left off every list.
##		- 20260727 JC: A conflicted tree is never committed. A pull whose autostash reapply conflicts exits 0, so 'git add --all' was marking the conflict resolved and committing the markers - then pushing them from sync.
##		- 20260727 JC: Review round: 'pr ok <n>' checks the PR's own branch for unpushed commits, not whichever branch you're standing on (gh force-deletes it either way); the default branch is resolved rather than assumed to be 'main', and commands refuse when it can't be told; 'release' refuses an invented version with nothing to release; 'br land' won't delete a leftover main/master; '--help' works after a command; a message may start with a dash; '--public' with '--private' is refused; credentialed URLs are masked on the execution line too; the identity probes actually cache now; 'br switch' onto the current branch stops previewing a park it doesn't do.
##		- 20260727 JC: Review round, smaller items: option and positional typos are usage errors, not crashes (no call-stack line); status and br list reject trailing arguments; 'gh pr create' is announced as it's actually run; the crash dump uses printf, so escape bytes from a filename or message stay inert; template leftovers out of the argument parser.
##		- 20260728 JC: An unreachable remote no longer fails the push. Commands that mean something locally (br create/hotfix/switch/land) skip it and say so; the ones that exist to publish (sync, pr create, pr ok, release) refuse up front instead. br land holds back the remote branch delete until the merge is published, and the hotfix back-merge falls back to the local default branch when origin's copy is the stale one.
##		- 20260728 JC: 'repo create' and 'repo connect' from a plain directory list the files they are about to publish, asked through a throwaway git dir outside the work tree so .gitignore is honored and nothing is written into the user's directory.
##		- 20260730 JC: The SSH identity line asked 'ssh -G' about the bare host, which answers with the OS login name when the target carries no user - so it named neither the connect user nor the account. It probes the connect target now, and leads with the account the key authenticates as, asked of the host rather than inferred from the key filename.
##		- 20260730 JC: Offline messages tell the truth: a park with nothing to push says so, the skipped-push warning names the branch it means (the command may move off it next), and an offline hotfix land names 'br switch <default>' then 'sync' - a bare 'sync' runs from dev after the back-merge and would have left the default branch stale on origin.
##		- 20260731 JC: Branch names display against the branch they land on ('dev :: feature/retries'); br create/hotfix name the branch they are about to make and where it comes from; the repo default gets its own line instead of a parenthetical, and br list states it before listing.
##		- 20260731 JC: The status block labels the branch you are on 'Current branch', so it reads against the 'Default branch' and 'New branch' lines beside it.
##		- 20260731 JC: br list runs in a repo whose default branch can't be told - like status it reports it unknown and lists anyway, which is what you need to see to fix it.
##		- 20260808 JC: Folder-based accounts: which GitHub account a command acts as follows the directory the repo lives in, read from a config file, resolved once per run and applied through the environment - so nothing is written to disk and a killed run strands nothing. GITSBY_ACCOUNT and a repo-local gitsby.ghAccount override it; with no config at all nothing changes.
##		- 20260808 JC: 'repo url' shows a remote's other spelling and converts between them. Https with the account's token is what makes an ssh key optional, so an ssh remote in a folder whose account holds a token is offered the switch once.
##		- 20260808 JC: 'account' lists what is configured and which rule this folder matches, naming where a token comes from rather than printing it. 'account apply' writes matching includeIf rules into the global git config, so plain git and gh follow the same folder rules; re-applying refreshes rather than accumulates, and a hand-written rule is left alone.
##		- 20260808 JC: 'raw git' and 'raw gh' hand everything after the tool name to it untouched, under the account this folder resolves to - the scripting surface. Nothing but those two can be reached that way.
##		- 20260808 JC: A local-path remote is compared as a path rather than as text. Git stores it in the platform's own spelling, so on Windows a re-run 'repo clone' and a 'repo connect' with a matching url each mistook the remote they already had for a different one.
##		- 20260809 JC: '--config' with an empty value is refused instead of falling back to the default file. A script expanding a variable that turned out empty would otherwise act as whichever account that file names.
##		- 20260812 JC: The token reaches git's credential helper whenever one is found, not only when it replaces a different active account. The helper sets aside any credential manager configured ahead of it, so where the two already matched git was left to authenticate over https with an empty password - the case that needs no configuration at all.
##		- 20260812 JC: A '#' after whitespace ends a config value, so a trailing comment is a comment there as well as at the start of a line. A rule that had swallowed its own comment could never match a directory, which reads exactly like no rule at all. Quote a value to keep a literal '#'.
##		- 20260812 JC: An account name is held to letters, digits, dot, dash and underscore. The name becomes a file under the include directory, so one carrying a path of its own sent 'account apply' to write somewhere else entirely.
##		- 20260812 JC: 'origin/HEAD' is healed only when there is nothing to read locally, and the passthrough no longer asks gh which account is active. Each was a round trip on every command - for something git 2.47 writes at clone, and for a name only the identity block prints.
##		- 20260812 JC: The identity line names an account asked for by name, including the bare login GITSBY_ACCOUNT accepts. It had asked whether some configured value was used, so a bare login selected the token and printed no line. An account only inferred from the remote owner is still not shown.
##		- 20260812 JC: Account rules are applied shortest path first, so git (last match wins) and gitsby (longest match wins) agree about a nested tree. Account names match whatever case you type. Every gitsby option works before "raw", which used to report an unknown command and name the wrong token. A --config naming a directory is refused, and an unusable include directory is reported rather than dumped as a shell error.
##		- 20260812 JC: The identity line says when an account was resolved but not applied, and the commands that push with git compare it against the key origin authenticates as - "sync" compared nothing at all. "account apply" also writes the https credential username. "br prune" asks git once per target ref rather than twice per branch, and a folder rule pointing at no directory is marked as one that can never match.
##		- 20260812 JC: "account.<name>.pathContains" matches a run of folder names anywhere in a path, so one config file can be synced between machines whose roots differ. Whole names only; more names is the more specific rule; an absolute "path" still wins. "account apply" emits it as a gitdir glob, which git handles natively.
##		- 20260813 JC: The publish preview's throwaway git dir survives an interrupt no longer - it is removed on the way out of the run, not only on the way out of the function that made it.
